/*
  Localizable.strings

  Every user-facing string in the app, keyed by its English text.

  SwiftUI localises a literal passed to Text, Button, Label and friends by
  treating the literal itself as the key, so extracting the copy here changed
  no call site and cannot change what renders: a key with no entry falls back
  to the literal. What it buys is that the copy can now be found, reviewed and
  translated in one place, which was impossible while it was scattered across
  eighty-odd files.

  The Android app keeps the same copy in res/values/strings.xml. Where the two
  differ it is because the platform control differs -- iOS splits an empty
  state into a title and a description, Android states it in one line -- and
  not because the product says different things on different phones.

  Keys are unique across the file: a word used on three screens is one entry,
  translated once. That is also why the shared block comes first.
*/

